<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cape Palmas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palmas</a> is a headland at the south-eastern end of Liberia, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>, where the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> turns east into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. In 1834 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland-state-colonization-society">Maryland State Colonization Society</a> founded a settlement there, which became the independent Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> in 1854 and joined Liberia in 1857. The town of Harper stands on the cape.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cape-palmas.jpg" alt="Cape Palmas" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wagner &amp; C., Lith., Philadelphia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cape_Palmas_etch.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The cape is a small, rocky peninsula joined to the mainland by a sandy isthmus. The estuary of the Hoffman River lies just to the west, and about 21 kilometres to the east the Cavalla River reaches the sea on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-coast">Côte d'Ivoire</a>. The International Hydrographic Organization takes the cape as the western limit of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-guinea">Gulf of Guinea</a>. Offshore lies Russwurm Island, joined to the peninsula by a breakwater, and a lighthouse warns ships of the many shoals nearby.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1458 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Prince Henry the Navigator</a> of Portugal sent his captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-gomes">Diogo Gomes</a> down the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> as far as this point, where the shore stops running south and turns east. Gomes named it Cabo das <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palma">Palmas</a>, the Cape of the Palms, and the river Rio das Palmas, later the Hoffman. The name appears on the Cantino planisphere of 1502, the earliest map known to carry it, at a time when Europeans called this stretch of coast the Malaguetta Coast.</p><p>In December 1831 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a> legislature appropriated US$10,000 for 26 years to carry free black people and former slaves from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland-state-colonization-society">Maryland State Colonization Society</a>, once a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, was formed for the purpose. It named its settlement Maryland in Africa on 12 February 1834. The colony became a state on 2 February 1841, and after independence in 1854 it was annexed to Liberia by treaty on 18 March 1857. Its first black governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-brown-russwurm">John Brown Russwurm</a>, gave his name to the island off the cape. The Episcopal missionary Elizabeth Mars Johnson Thomson taught there from 1835 to 1862.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city of Harper, founded by the society in 1835, runs inland along the Hoffman estuary and has a small harbour; a white masonic lodge hall with a large golden orb on its roof is a landmark seen from the sea. The name Cape Palmas is often used for the whole of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland-county">Maryland County</a> and for Harper, its county seat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cape Palmas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under agents such as Jehudi Ashmun the colony survived disease and conflict with neighbouring peoples, and it was joined by other settlements founded by state colonisation societies, including Maryland in Liberia at <b>Cape Palmas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They bought land at <b>Cape Palmas</b> from Grebo rulers and founded the town of Harper.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-maryland">Republic of Maryland</a></li></ul></section>
